@prefix pat: <http://seanchatmangpt.github.io/packs/pack-authoring#> .
@prefix rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .

# pack-authoring-pack: teaches (Diataxis docs) and scaffolds (real files) the
# ggen pack-authoring pattern proven by gh-terraform-pack in this session and
# generalized by crates/ggen-engine/tests/support (scaffold_pack,
# assert_idempotent, assert_gate_refuses). Two concerns, two classes:
#
#   pat:DiataxisDoc  — this pack's OWN docs (fixed individuals below, one per
#                      Diataxis quadrant, verbatim content).
#   pat:NewPackSpec  — declared by the CONSUMER (one individual per new pack
#                      they want scaffolded); this pack's templates project
#                      each spec into a real pack.toml/ontology.ttl/
#                      templates/gates/tests skeleton. No pat:NewPackSpec
#                      individuals are declared HERE — a bare `ggen sync`
#                      against this pack alone renders only the docs.

# --- Diataxis docs -----------------------------------------------------

pat:DiataxisDoc a rdfs:Class ;
    rdfs:label "One page of this pack's own pack-authoring documentation, in Diataxis's tutorial/how-to/reference/explanation split." .

pat:TutorialDoc a pat:DiataxisDoc ;
    pat:docPath "docs/pack-authoring/tutorial.md" ;
    pat:content """# Tutorial: build your first ggen pack

This walks through creating one working pack from nothing, end to end. By
the finish you will have run `ggen sync` and watched a real file land on
disk from an RDF fact.

## 1. Lay out the directory

```
packs/hello-pack/
  pack.toml
  ontology.ttl
  templates/
    greeting.txt.tmpl
```

## 2. `pack.toml`

```toml
[pack]
name = "hello-pack"
version = "0.1.0"
description = "Says hello, from RDF."
```

## 3. `ontology.ttl` — one class, one individual

```turtle
@prefix hp: <http://example.org/hello-pack#> .

hp:Greeting a hp:GreetingClass ;
    hp:text "Hello from ggen." .
```

## 4. `templates/greeting.txt.tmpl` — one template

```
---
to: "output/greeting.txt"
sparql:
  row: |
    PREFIX hp: <http://example.org/hello-pack#>
    SELECT ?text WHERE { hp:Greeting hp:text ?text . } ORDER BY ?text
---
{{ row[0].text }}
```

Templates never synthesize content at render time — they echo a literal
already stored in the ontology. If the string isn't in the RDF, it can't
appear in the output. This is the same "verbatim, not synthesized" contract
`gh-terraform-pack` uses for every one of its 40+ templates.

## 5. Wire a consumer project and sync

```toml
# consumer/ggen.toml
[project]
name = "hello-consumer"

[ontology]
source = "ontology.ttl"

[packs]
hello-pack = { path = "../hello-pack" }

[templates]
dir = "templates"
```

```bash
ggen sync run
cat output/greeting.txt   # "Hello from ggen."
```

Run it again — nothing changes. That's `ggen`'s idempotency guarantee, and
it's the first thing your pack's Chicago-TDD test should prove (see the
how-to below).

## Next

- **`how-to-add-a-gate.md`** — refuse bad facts before they generate bad files.
- **`how-to-write-a-chicago-e2e-test.md`** — prove your pack works, for real.
- **`reference.md`** — the full file-layout contract.
- **`explanation.md`** — why packs are shaped this way.
- **`advanced-practices.md`** — optional practices proven in one pack
  (`dspy-pack`) so far, with explicit fit/don't-fit guidance before you
  adopt any of them.""" .

pat:HowToGateDoc a pat:DiataxisDoc ;
    pat:docPath "docs/pack-authoring/how-to-add-a-gate.md" ;
    pat:content """# How to: add an admission gate

A gate is a `gates/*.rq` SPARQL `SELECT` that returns rows for every
violation. A non-empty result set refuses the sync — before Extract,
Render, or Write ever run, so a refused sync leaves no partial output,
`ggen.lock`, or receipt on disk.

## The required-properties idiom

Every existing pack (`gh-terraform-pack`, `praxis-core-pack`, ...) uses this
exact shape for `gates/010_required.rq`:

```sparql
# MESSAGE: every hp:Greeting must have a non-empty hp:text. Any row = a
# subject missing its required property.
PREFIX hp: <http://example.org/hello-pack#>
SELECT ?s ?missing WHERE {
  {
    ?s a hp:GreetingClass . BIND(hp:text AS ?missing)
  }
  FILTER NOT EXISTS { ?s ?missing ?any }
}
ORDER BY ?s ?missing
```

- The leading `# MESSAGE:` comment is not decorative — it is surfaced
  verbatim in the refusal error, so write it for the human who trips the
  gate, not for yourself.
- One `UNION` branch per (class, required-property) pair. Add a branch, not
  a new file, as your ontology grows — one `010_required.rq` per pack keeps
  the "what must every individual carry" contract in one place.
- Gates run against the UNION graph (this pack's facts plus every other
  pack's plus the consumer project's own `ontology.ttl`), so a violation can
  come from any source — the refusal message says so explicitly; don't
  narrow your gate's `FILTER` to assume only your own individuals exist.

## Policy gates (cross-individual, not just "is this field present")

A second gate file, conventionally `gates/030_policy.rq` (see
`gh-terraform-pack/gates/030_policy.rq`), expresses rules that reference
MORE than one individual — "critical-tier repos need >=2 reviewers",
"secrets must be vault-backed", etc. Same `SELECT ?s ?detail` /
non-empty-refuses shape; just a richer `WHERE` clause.

## Proving a gate actually fires

Documentation and structural review are not proof a gate refuses what it
claims to. Use `support::assert_gate_refuses` (see
`how-to-write-a-chicago-e2e-test.md`) to feed the gate a hand-crafted bad
fact and assert the sync is refused BY NAME — citing your gate's filename
fragment in the error.""" .

pat:HowToTestDoc a pat:DiataxisDoc ;
    pat:docPath "docs/pack-authoring/how-to-write-a-chicago-e2e-test.md" ;
    pat:content """# How to: write a Chicago-TDD e2e test for your pack

ggen's own convention (`crates/ggen-engine/tests/*_pack_e2e.rs`, one file
per pack) is real filesystem, real `sync()`, real assertions on real file
content — no mocks, no test doubles, matching this repo's Chicago TDD
policy. `crates/ggen-engine/tests/support/mod.rs` gives you the four
building blocks so you don't hand-roll scaffolding per pack.

## The four-part pattern

```rust
mod support;
use support::{scaffold_pack, assert_idempotent, assert_gate_refuses};
use std::path::{Path, PathBuf};

fn packs_dir() -> PathBuf {
    Path::new(env!("CARGO_MANIFEST_DIR")).join("../../packs")
}

#[test]
fn hello_pack_generates_and_is_idempotent() {
    // 1. SCAFFOLD: copy the real pack + a minimal consumer into a TempDir.
    let (_dir, project) = scaffold_pack(&packs_dir().join("hello-pack"));

    // 2. GENERATE + ASSERT REAL CONTENT: run the real sync(), then read the
    //    real file it wrote and assert on its exact content — not just
    //    that it exists.
    ggen_engine::sync::sync(&project, ggen_engine::sync::SyncOptions {
        dry_run: false, ..Default::default()
    }).expect("sync");
    let greeting = std::fs::read_to_string(project.join("output/greeting.txt"))
        .expect("greeting.txt");
    assert_eq!(greeting.trim(), "Hello from ggen.");

    // 3. IDEMPOTENCY: a second sync must write nothing and leave ggen.lock
    //    byte-identical.
    assert_idempotent(&project);
}

#[test]
fn hello_pack_gate_refuses_missing_text() {
    let (_dir, project) = scaffold_pack(&packs_dir().join("hello-pack"));
    ggen_engine::sync::sync(&project, ggen_engine::sync::SyncOptions {
        dry_run: false, ..Default::default()
    }).expect("baseline sync");

    // 4. GATE SABOTAGE: overwrite the CONSUMER's ontology.ttl with a bad
    //    fact and assert the sync refuses, citing the gate by name, with
    //    no partial ggen.lock left behind.
    assert_gate_refuses(
        &project,
        "@prefix hp: <http://example.org/hello-pack#> .\\n\\
         hp:Sabotage a hp:GreetingClass .\\n",
        "010_required",
    );
}
```

## Why all four steps, not fewer

- Skip (2)'s content assertion and a template that silently renders empty
  still "passes" a bare existence check — assert the string, not just the
  file.
- Skip (3) and a template that regenerates differently every run (e.g. an
  unstable iteration order) goes undetected until a consumer's CI flags
  unexpected diffs.
- Skip (4) and a gate that never actually fires (a typo'd predicate name, a
  `FILTER` that's always false) sits in the repo as decoration, not
  admission control — see `.claude/rules/coding-agent-mistakes.md`'s
  "Decorative Completion" mistake class.""" .

pat:ReferenceDoc a pat:DiataxisDoc ;
    pat:docPath "docs/pack-authoring/reference.md" ;
    pat:content """# Reference: ggen pack file layout

| Path | Required | Contract |
|---|---|---|
| `pack.toml` | yes | `[pack]` table: `name`, `version` (semver), `description`. No other top-level tables. |
| `ontology.ttl` | yes | Turtle. One or more `@prefix` declarations plus RDF classes/individuals — the pack's facts, unioned with every other pack's and the consumer's own `ontology.ttl` at sync time. |
| `templates/*.tmpl` | at least 1 | YAML frontmatter (`---`/`---`) then a Tera body. Frontmatter: `to:` (output path, project-relative, may interpolate `{{ var }}` from a SPARQL row) and either `sparql.row:` (exactly one row expected) or `sparql.rows:` (zero or more rows, looped with `{% for row in rows %}`). |
| `gates/*.rq` | no | SPARQL `SELECT`. Non-empty result set refuses the sync before Extract/Render/Write. Files run in filename sort order (`010_`, `020_`, ... convention). |

## Template `to:` fan-out rule

If `to:` contains a `{{ variable }}`, the engine writes **one output file
per query row** — a query returning N rows for the same resolved `to:`
path is a collision (`FM-WRITE-008`), not an aggregation. To collapse
multiple facts into one file per key, `GROUP BY` the key and
`GROUP_CONCAT` the varying field in the SPARQL — see
`resource_doc.tmpl`/`backend_catalog_doc.tmpl` in `gh-terraform-pack` for
the real, working idiom.

## Gate file shape

```sparql
# MESSAGE: <human-readable statement of the invariant, surfaced verbatim
# in the refusal error>
PREFIX <prefix>: <...>
SELECT ?s ?missing WHERE {
  { ?s a <Class> . BIND(<requiredProp> AS ?missing) } UNION { ... }
  FILTER NOT EXISTS { ?s ?missing ?any }
}
ORDER BY ?s ?missing
```

## Chicago-TDD test shape

One `tests/<pack>_pack_e2e.rs` per pack, using
`crates/ggen-engine/tests/support::{scaffold_pack, assert_idempotent,
assert_gate_refuses}` — see `how-to-write-a-chicago-e2e-test.md`.""" .

pat:ExplanationDoc a pat:DiataxisDoc ;
    pat:docPath "docs/pack-authoring/explanation.md" ;
    pat:content """# Explanation: why packs are shaped this way

## RDF is the source of truth, not the generated files

`ggen`'s core discipline (see the repo's own `CLAUDE.md`: "RDF is Truth —
edit `.specify/*.ttl`, never the generated `.md`") extends to every pack: an
`ontology.ttl` fact is what's real; a `.tf`/`.md`/`.rs` file under `docs/`
or `infra/` is a *projection* of that fact, regenerated on demand. If a
generated file says something the ontology doesn't, the file is wrong, not
the ontology — this is why `ggen sync` is idempotent and why a second sync
producing a diff is treated as a bug, not a feature.

## Templates echo, they never synthesize

Every template in this pack (and in `gh-terraform-pack`, which this
convention is modeled on) either echoes a literal `hclBody`/`content`
string already stored in the ontology, or fills a small number of named
holes (`{{ name }}`, `{{ className }}`) from a spec individual's own
properties. No template computes new content the ontology doesn't already
assert. This is a deliberate constraint, not a missing feature: it keeps
"what will this pack generate" answerable by reading the RDF, without
running the template engine to find out.

## Gates are admission control, not test assertions

A `gates/*.rq` file runs at sync time, against real consumer data, and
refuses BEFORE any file is written — it protects every future sync, not
just the one your test happens to run. A Chicago-TDD test then separately
PROVES the gate fires (via `assert_gate_refuses`), because a gate that
silently never matches anything is indistinguishable, by inspection alone,
from a gate that works — the same "decorative completion" risk this repo's
`coding-agent-mistakes.md` names for any patch.

## Why a shared test harness, not one bespoke scaffold per pack

`crates/ggen-engine/tests/support/mod.rs` exists because
`gh_terraform_pack_e2e.rs` and the older `pack_e2e.rs` had independently
reinvented the same `TempDir`/`copy_tree`/scaffold code. Two copies of the
same logic drift the moment one is edited and the other isn't — the
`coding-agent-mistakes.md` "reduce drift" half of this repo's strongest
rule. One shared, tested implementation is the version every new pack's
`<pack>_pack_e2e.rs` should build on, including the ones this pack itself
scaffolds.""" .

pat:ErrcDoc a pat:DiataxisDoc ;
    pat:docPath "docs/pack-authoring/errc-testing-pattern.md" ;
    pat:content """# ERRC: the pack-testing pattern

Blue Ocean Strategy's Eliminate–Reduce–Raise–Create grid, applied to how
this repo tests `packs/*` — grounded in `scripts/ci/guard-pack-e2e-coverage.sh`'s
own real output (81 packs total; 15 have real testable surface — `gates/*.rq`
or `templates/*.tmpl` — and no e2e coverage anywhere, checked by grepping
every file under `crates/ggen-engine/tests/` for the pack's own directory-name
string, not by guessing test filenames), not a generic template. That number
has moved twice already, each time downward for a real reason, not by
adjustment: an initial hand survey said 67 (it only looked at files named
`*_pack_e2e.rs`, missing real coverage some packs get from broader
multi-pack tests like `composed_packs_e2e.rs` — `tcps-release-pack`, 85
templates, the single largest pack, turned out to already be covered
there); the corrected gate script then found 22; running its own
`pat:ExistingPackStub` generator against the cheapest uncovered packs
closed 7 more for real (5 then 2), landing at 15 — with 5 of the remaining
15 already attempted and found to need real per-pack domain-ontology work
(their gates correctly refuse an empty consumer ontology) or a genuine
non-determinism bug fix, not more stub-generation. Living document:
regenerate it via `ggen sync` rather than hand-editing, the same as every
other page here.

## Eliminate

- **Per-file scaffold duplication.** Before `crates/ggen-engine/tests/
  support/mod.rs` existed, `copy_tree`/`scaffold`/`read` were reimplemented,
  byte-for-byte identically, in 9 separate `*_pack_e2e.rs` files. All 9 now
  import the shared versions; ~150 lines of duplicate boilerplate removed,
  zero behavior change (verified: every migrated file's tests pass
  identically before and after).
- **"No test at all" as the silent default for a new pack.** Before this
  pack existed, authoring a new pack meant either copying an existing
  `*_pack_e2e.rs` by hand or, more often, shipping with zero e2e coverage —
  which is how 15 of 81 packs remain untested today. `pack-authoring-pack`
  always emits a stub test alongside a new pack's scaffold; skipping
  coverage now takes an active choice, not an absent one.

## Reduce

- **Idempotency + gate-sabotage boilerplate**, from ~20-40 hand-written
  lines per pack to two function calls (`assert_idempotent`,
  `assert_gate_refuses`) — already true for every migrated file.
- **Bespoke scaffold-shape reinvention.** Two real variants surfaced by
  reading all 7 remaining files, not invented up front:
  `scaffold_pack_with_ontology` (pre-populated consumer ontology — used by
  `tai_rebuild_pack_e2e.rs`) and `read_json` (JSON-parsing read — same
  file). Genuinely different shapes (nested pack paths, `[law] reflexive =
  true`, `extra_ontologies`, declarative-rules `[[generation.rules]]`,
  `CliHarness` subprocess vs. in-process `sync()`) were deliberately left
  local rather than forced through one wrapper — a wrapper that silently
  changes what is being tested is worse than the duplication it replaces.

## Raise

- **A visible, enforced coverage floor.** `scripts/ci/guard-pack-e2e-coverage.sh`
  is now a real ratchet, not just a report: it hard-fails `pre-commit` the
  moment the real uncovered count exceeds the checked-in baseline
  (`.specify/pack-e2e-coverage-baseline.txt`, currently `15`) — a pack
  losing coverage or a new pack shipping real testable surface with none
  is now a build break, not a silent regression. It does not demand the
  current 15 close first (several are legitimately blocked on real
  per-pack domain work this pattern cannot manufacture) — proven as a real
  gate, not asserted: lowering the baseline file by one and re-running the
  script was checked to actually fail before the correct value was
  restored.
- **Assertion quality as the norm, not the exception.** Every migrated and
  generated test asserts real content substrings (a `#[test]` that only
  checks `sync()` returned `Ok` is not yet a Chicago-TDD proof, per
  `how-to-write-a-chicago-e2e-test.md`) — the `chicago-tdd-tools-pack`
  "proof of the generated proof" idiom generalized to every pack, not just
  the one pack that originated it.

## Create

- **The coverage-tracking ratchet gate** (above) — makes the gap a number
  that can only shrink on purpose, self-corrects when the measurement
  itself improves (67→22, see above), and now actually enforces the floor
  instead of only reporting it (22→15, closed by the stub generator below).
- **Ranked, real stub generation** for the highest-surface uncovered packs
  (`pat:ExistingPackStub` + its template) — closes a bounded, verified
  subset per pass rather than claiming the full remaining count in one,
  which would trade real coverage for the appearance of it.
- **This document itself**, regenerated by the same `ggen sync` that
  produces every other page here — an ERRC analysis that goes stale the
  moment the inventory changes is worse than no analysis; this one is
  re-derivable, not a one-time memo.""" .

pat:AdvancedPracticesDoc a pat:DiataxisDoc ;
    pat:docPath "docs/pack-authoring/advanced-practices.md" ;
    pat:content """# Advanced practices: beyond the core layout

The core file layout (`reference.md`) and the four-part Chicago-TDD pattern
(`how-to-write-a-chicago-e2e-test.md`) are load-bearing for every pack. The
five practices below are **not** part of that mandatory core — a 2026-08-11
survey of `packs/*` found each one real and proven in exactly one pack
(`dspy-pack`, verified against the real installed `dspy 3.1.3` package
across a multi-session build-out), but not yet followed anywhere else, and
not always applicable. Each entry states what it is, when it genuinely
fits, and — as important — when it doesn't, so adopting one isn't cargo
culting `dspy-pack`'s specific circumstances onto a pack that doesn't share
them.

## 1. Zero-individuals, vocabulary-only shipping

`dspy-pack/pack.toml` states this as a load-bearing property, not a
description: "`ontology.ttl` ships zero individuals, so composing this pack
cannot inject any signature, module, or optimizer into a consumer's
generated output." The pack is pure RDFS/OWL vocabulary (classes,
properties, closed-value enumerations) — every actual fact comes from the
consumer's own `.ttl`.

**Fits**: packs whose entire purpose is a reusable *vocabulary* for
consumer-authored facts — a typed schema other projects populate.

**Does not fit**: packs whose value IS the shipped facts — `fortune5-
required-capabilities-pack`, `wasm4pm-facts-pack`, and most of the
domain-modeling packs in this marketplace legitimately ship real
individuals (control catalogs, admitted breed/algorithm lists, transcribed
source vocabularies). Forcing those to zero individuals would delete the
pack's actual content, not improve it.

## 2. Evidence + coverage drift guard (`evidence/*.ttl` + a checker script)

`dspy-pack/evidence/dspy-source-coverage.ttl` records one
`dspyaud:AdmittedKind` fact per real `dspy` source class the pack's gate
admits, and `dspy-pack/scripts/coverage_check.py` re-parses the live gate's
`VALUES` lists (regex-style, not by trusting a cached list) and cross-checks
them against both the ledger AND the real installed target library via
`inspect.signature`/source inspection — failing loudly on drift in either
direction (gate admits something the ledger doesn't know about, or vice
versa).

**Fits**: any pack that models a *real, versioned external API* the pack
doesn't control (a library, a CLI, a published schema) — the exact
situation `dspy-pack` is in with the installed `dspy` package. The value is
catching upstream API drift (a new dspy release renaming a constructor
kwarg) before it silently breaks generated code.

**Does not fit**: packs modeling a domain vocabulary with no external
"real" implementation to drift against (there's no live oracle to check
`fortune5-architecture-pack`'s capability taxonomy against) — for those,
the SHACL/gate layer alone is the right level of rigor.

## 3. `VALUES`-table gate consolidation

`dspy-pack/gates/010_admission.rq`'s own header comment documents a real,
measured `praxis-graphlaw` SPARQL-engine limit: the evaluator stack-
overflows somewhere between ~139 and ~158 `UNION` branches in one gate
file's algebra tree. The fix used throughout that gate: replace many
single-purpose `UNION` branches (one per property/kind check) with a single
`VALUES (?type ?property) { (...) (...) ... }` block, collapsing ~10
branches into 1.

**Fits**: any gate file that is growing past roughly 100 `UNION` branches,
or is likely to (a pack admitting many kinds × many required properties,
like an optimizer/module taxonomy). Apply proactively once a gate has more
than 2-3 kind-conditional blocks with a shared branch shape, not only after
hitting the crash.

**Does not fit**: a small gate (the common case — most packs' `gates/
010_required.rq` are under 10 branches, per `reference.md`'s idiom) has no
reason to adopt the less-obviously-readable `VALUES` form pre-emptively;
plain `UNION` is more legible at that scale and the engine limit is nowhere
close.

## 4. Real-source signature verification

Every admitted class/kind in `dspy-pack` was verified against the
*installed* `dspy` package via `inspect.signature()`/`inspect.getsource()`
and, where feasible, actual object construction and execution — not
against documentation prose or memory. This found three real, otherwise-
undocumented upstream `dspy` defects (an unexported, broken
`SignatureOptimizer` shim; `AvatarOptimizer` calling a removed
`TypedPredictor`; `BetterTogether`'s narrower-than-advertised runtime
optimizer whitelist), each recorded as a caveat directly on the relevant
ontology property.

**Fits**: any pack targeting a real, installable/runnable external library
or tool, where "verified" can mean "ran code against the real thing," not
just "read the docs" — this repo's own `Evidence-First Principle`
(`CLAUDE.md`) already states this as a general discipline; `dspy-pack` is
simply the pack that has applied it most completely to its own admitted
vocabulary.

**Does not fit**: packs with no runnable external target to verify against
(pure domain/policy vocabularies) — there, the analogous discipline is
citing the real source document/ticket/RFC a rule comes from, per
`reference.md`'s existing citation norm.

## 5. Dated, incrementally-appended `pack.toml` description as changelog

`dspy-pack/pack.toml`'s `description` field is a long, multi-paragraph,
dated narrative — each real capability addition appends a new dated
paragraph (e.g. "2026-08-10: composition/retrieval/KNN/Parallel/Ensemble/
KNNFewShot support added...") rather than being rewritten to a fresh
static summary each time. Read together with `git log -- pack.toml`, it
gives a consumer a single-file view of what changed and when, without
needing to reconstruct pack history from commit messages.

**Fits**: packs under active, incremental evolution where the coverage
surface itself is the interesting fact to track over time (how many
kinds/classes are admitted, what was excluded and why, what got resolved).

**Does not fit**: a pack with a stable, rarely-changing scope is better
served by `reference.md`'s existing norm — a short, current, static
one-paragraph description — since an ever-growing changelog-as-description
mostly adds noise once the pack stops evolving month to month.

## Source

`~/autofde-lab/docs/planning/fortune5-k8s-state-space/ROADMAP.md` prompted
this survey by naming `dspy-pack`'s own gate as a precedent a proposed
`k8s-pack` should NOT repeat (its `str`/`int`/`float`/`bool`-only field
typing, with `PydanticModelField`→`PydanticModel` nesting structurally
forbidden) — surfacing that `dspy-pack` had already solved several other
problems (drift, engine limits, verification rigor) worth generalizing
even though that one specific limitation should not be.""" .

# --- New-pack scaffold spec (consumer-declared) -------------------------

pat:NewPackSpec a rdfs:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Declared by a CONSUMER project (not by this pack) — one individual per new pack to scaffold. Required: pat:packName, pat:description, pat:className, pat:prefixName, pat:requiredProp." .

# --- Existing-pack stub spec (consumer-declared) -------------------------
# Retrofits a generic scaffold+idempotency Chicago-TDD stub test onto a pack
# that ALREADY EXISTS on disk (packs/<packName>/), without touching any of
# its real files — deliberately narrower than pat:NewPackSpec: no
# className/prefixName/requiredProp, since fabricating those for a pack this
# generator has never read would be guessing, not generating. The stub's
# only real assertion is that a real sync() against the real pack succeeds
# and is idempotent; a gate-sabotage test needs the pack's own real
# required-property name and is left as a named TODO pointing at the pack's
# own gates/010_required.rq, not invented here.

pat:ExistingPackStub a rdfs:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Declared by a CONSUMER (not by this pack) — one individual per EXISTING pack to retrofit a generic e2e stub onto. Required: pat:packName only." .
